{:id "006-private-personas"
 ;; A persona has been one thing since 003: a *public address*. `/:persona-id`
 ;; serves anybody, and every GET under `/api/personas/:name/...` is
 ;; unauthenticated by design — "personalist serves what a visitor of
 ;; personalist.org sees", as server/describe-handler puts it. This column is
 ;; where that stops being true of every persona.
 ;;
 ;; ONE FLAG, AND IT MEANS UNREADABLE RATHER THAN UNLISTED. An index a persona is
 ;; missing from, still served to anyone who guesses the address, would be a
 ;; promise this app does not keep — and this app is about provenance and trust
 ;; before it is about anything else. So `private = 1` takes the persona out of
 ;; the public list *and* answers its identities, history, relations and search
 ;; with the same 404 an id nobody has ever used gets. The existence of a private
 ;; persona is part of what is private; a 403 would confirm it and let a stranger
 ;; map an account by probing ids.
 ;;
 ;; Nothing about *writing* is touched. `wrap-auth` already answers "may you
 ;; write this persona?" and a private one is written by exactly the people who
 ;; could write it yesterday. This is a read rule.
 :up [;; SQLite refuses NOT NULL on an added column only when there is no
      ;; default; with one it is legal, and the column is written into every
      ;; existing row as it is added. So the constraint and the retrofit are one
      ;; statement, exactly as 005's `author` was, and every persona that exists
      ;; when this runs is stamped public.
      ;;
      ;; **Public is what they have been**, so unlike 005 this is not recording
      ;; anybody's answer about the past — it is the only value that could be
      ;; correct. Every one of these addresses has been served to the world since
      ;; the day it was made; a migration that guessed `private` would be
      ;; inventing a history in which they were not.
      ;;
      ;; **And unlike 005, the default is fine as a write path too.** A forgotten
      ;; `author` would have been a false claim about a person, in the dangerous
      ;; direction. A forgotten `private` says the persona is what personas were
      ;; before this migration, and the fact is mutable: the owner flips it from
      ;; the profile page whenever they like, which is not true of who wrote a
      ;; line. So `ds/add-persona` takes it in a trailing opts map rather than as
      ;; a required positional argument.
      ;;
      ;; No table rebuild, which is also why this is one statement. `personas`
      ;; has a TEXT primary key and no AUTOINCREMENT, so there was no
      ;; sqlite_sequence high-water mark to lose even in 003's rebuild, and there
      ;; is certainly none here.
      "ALTER TABLE personas ADD COLUMN private INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0"]

 ;; The column goes and nothing else does — ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN has been
 ;; legal since SQLite 3.35 and the driver here is well past that, as 005 already
 ;; relies on.
 ;;
 ;; **It publishes every private persona.** There is one column holding the fact
 ;; and dropping it cannot mean anything else: after a rollback each of these
 ;; addresses is served to the world again, and re-migrating afterwards stamps
 ;; them public a second time rather than remembering what they were. That is the
 ;; honest consequence of rolling back the only place the fact is kept, and it is
 ;; the same shape as 005 destroying every attribution on the way down — but it
 ;; is worse in one respect, which is worth saying plainly: 005's rollback loses
 ;; a record, this one *discloses* what somebody chose to keep to themselves.
 ;; Make the personas public by hand first if a rollback is really what you want,
 ;; so that the disclosure is somebody's decision rather than a side effect.
 :down ["ALTER TABLE personas DROP COLUMN private"]
 :transactions false}
